WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.050
Film Courage: Is there a specific structure
you use in your screenplays?

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.780
Gary Goldstein: Yeah.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:14.230
Generally I defer to the classic storytelling
structure which is the 3-act structure, the

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:25.900
different series of movements across the whole
script, I break it down into 3 acts, 4 quarters,

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.039
12 sequences.

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:30.570
I try to mix it up a little.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:35.170
But I’m a big believer in drawing inside
the lines and then messing it up.

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:43.309
At least knowing that you’ve got your framework
the way that most stories are told best and

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:47.719
most effectively and you can kind of mess
it up and smudge it up and put your stamp

00:00:47.719 --> 00:00:48.800
on it, you know.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.380
I’m not such a big believer that what is
supposed to happen on page 60, happens exactly

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:54.380
on page 60.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:59.370
Well if it’s page 58 or 63 or if it’s
based upon the length of the script, page

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:00.370
42.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:08.200
As long as the movements or the proportion
of your story is right, the movements are

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.670
kind of traditional…not traditional, classical
storytelling.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:16.770
Because tradition always sounds like you’re
in a box.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.549
That to me is important.

00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:19.759
But I’m a big believer in structure.

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:25.430
I believe you get really found out why your
story isn’t working when you kind of don’t

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:30.340
follow some kind of classic storytelling structure.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:36.329
Film Courage: So going back to when you were
at the writer’s bootcamp, how would you

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:40.819
know if a story was great, but it just needed
to have more structure infused in it?

00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:43.649
Gary Goldstein: Well, I believe you can structure
any story.

00:01:43.649 --> 00:01:52.859
You have to have enough of a way to expand
that story into a full movie.

00:01:52.859 --> 00:01:55.840
You know, sometimes you watch a movie and
you go “Nothing happened in that movie.”

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.210
Or “What a bunch of nothing.”

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.920
It was really because there may not have been
enough story.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:06.899
My very first screenplay, I had three different
ideas for scripts and I ended up putting all

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:09.340
three together and I found my story.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.920
Individually each story may not have been
enough.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.310
Some how or another, all three of those ideas
together just sort of fit like this puzzle

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.540
and I was able to create this script.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.390
So there is that approach to it.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.870
But basically, you know a good story when
you hear it or you know the beginnings of

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:31.819
a good story or a story that has potential
or an interesting character.

00:02:31.819 --> 00:02:33.750
A lot of stories are just set up.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.459
It’s like “Blah, blah, blah, blah, blah…”

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:37.930
“Well, that’s great.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:38.930
But what happens then?”

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:40.579
Because that’s only taking me through the
first act.

00:02:40.579 --> 00:02:44.990
The second act is always the most difficult
act if you don’t know where you are going.

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:47.390
And most anybody can write the first act of
a script.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.840
But it’s where it goes from there that’s
tricky.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:53.640
Sometimes an idea might just be all set-up
and there’s nothing beyond that.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.200
But if you take another idea and pair it and
another idea and bring it in and kind of layer

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.190
it, then you may have more of that story.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:02.290
It’s about finding the story.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.069
Like I said, you can structure anything.

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:09.459
You can create a structure as long as there’s
enough story and finding that story, which

00:03:09.459 --> 00:03:10.280
is the challenge.

